  ('reasoning', 'Puzzles', 2, 'There are 25 horses and a track that races 5 at a time. There is no stopwatch, so a race only tells you the finishing order. What is the fewest races needed to find the single fastest horse?', array['6', '5', '7', '25'], 0, 'Race 5 groups of 5, then race the 5 group winners. The winner of that 6th race is the fastest.', 55, 'curated_puzzles', null, null, null),
  ('reasoning', 'Puzzles', 3, 'There are 25 horses and a track that races 5 at a time. There is no stopwatch, so a race only tells you the finishing order. What is the fewest races needed to find the 3 fastest horses?', array['8', '7', '6', '11'], 1, 'Race 5 groups of 5 (5 races), then race the group winners (race 6); its winner is the fastest. Only 5 horses can still be 2nd or 3rd: the 2nd and 3rd of race 6, the 2nd and 3rd from the fastest horse''s group, and the 2nd from the runner-up''s group. Race them (race 7).', 75, 'curated_puzzles', null, null, null),
  ('reasoning', 'Puzzles', 3, '100 doors are all closed. On pass 1 you toggle every door, on pass 2 every 2nd door, on pass 3 every 3rd door, and so on up to pass 100. How many doors are open at the end?', array['25', '1', '10', '50'], 2, 'A door is toggled once for each of its factors. Only perfect squares have an odd number of factors, so doors 1, 4, 9, …, 100 end up open: 10 doors.', 75, 'curated_puzzles', null, null, null),
  ('reasoning', 'Puzzles', 3, '100 doors start closed. On pass k (k = 1 to 100) you toggle every k-th door. Which of these doors is open at the end?', array['Door 50', 'Door 48', 'Door 64', 'Door 99'], 2, '64 is a perfect square (8 × 8), so it has an odd number of factors and is toggled an odd number of times. 50, 48 and 99 are not squares, so they end closed.', 75, 'curated_puzzles', null, null, null),
  ('reasoning', 'Puzzles', 3, 'You have 2 identical eggs and a 100-floor building. An egg breaks if dropped from a certain floor or higher. In the worst case, what is the fewest drops needed to find that floor?', array['7', '14', '10', '50'], 1, 'With d drops, drop the first egg from floor d, then d + (d − 1), and so on, checking the gaps with the second egg. That covers d(d + 1)/2 floors, and the smallest d with d(d + 1)/2 ≥ 100 is 14.', 75, 'curated_puzzles', null, null, null),
  ('reasoning', 'Puzzles', 3, 'Two ropes each take exactly 60 minutes to burn, but they burn unevenly, and you can only light their ends. Which of these times can you measure exactly?', array['45 minutes', '50 minutes', '40 minutes', '20 minutes'], 0, 'Light rope A at both ends and rope B at one end. A burns out at 30 minutes; then light B''s other end. Its remaining half burns from both ends in 15 minutes, giving 45 in total.', 75, 'curated_puzzles', null, null, null),
  ('reasoning', 'Puzzles', 2, 'Three switches outside a closed room control three bulbs inside. You can flip the switches as you like but enter the room only once. How do you match every switch to its bulb?', array['Switch one on, then go in and look', 'Leave one switch on for a few minutes, switch it off, switch on another, then go in and touch the bulbs', 'It cannot be done in one visit', 'Switch all three on, then go in'], 1, 'The lit bulb belongs to the switch that is on. The warm, dark bulb belongs to the switch you turned off. The cold, dark bulb belongs to the switch you never touched.', 55, 'curated_puzzles', null, null, null),
  ('reasoning', 'Puzzles', 2, 'You have 8 identical-looking balls. One is slightly heavier. Using a balance scale, what is the fewest weighings that always finds the heavy ball?', array['3', '2', '4', '1'], 1, 'Weigh 3 against 3. If they balance, weigh the last 2 against each other. If not, take the heavier 3 and weigh 1 against 1: the heavier side wins, or the ball left out if they balance.', 55, 'curated_puzzles', null, null, null),
  ('reasoning', 'Puzzles', 2, 'There are 27 coins and one is a lighter fake. With a balance scale, what is the fewest weighings that always finds the fake?', array['5', '3', '9', '4'], 1, 'Each weighing splits the suspects into three equal groups and shows which group holds the fake: 27 → 9 → 3 → 1, so 3 weighings.', 55, 'curated_puzzles', null, null, null),
  ('reasoning', 'Puzzles', 3, 'One of 1,000 bottles is poisoned, and the poison takes exactly 24 hours to act. You have only 24 hours. What is the fewest testers needed to find the poisoned bottle?', array['100', '500', '10', '1,000'], 2, 'Number the bottles in binary. Tester k sips from every bottle whose k-th bit is 1. Who falls ill spells out the poisoned bottle''s number, and 10 bits cover up to 1,024 bottles.', 75, 'curated_puzzles', null, null, null),
  ('reasoning', 'Puzzles', 3, 'Four people must cross a bridge at night with one torch, which must be carried on every crossing. At most two cross at a time, at the slower person''s pace. They take 1, 2, 5 and 10 minutes. What is the shortest total time?', array['18 minutes', '17 minutes', '21 minutes', '19 minutes'], 1, '1 and 2 cross (2), 1 returns (1), 5 and 10 cross (10), 2 returns (2), 1 and 2 cross (2): 2 + 1 + 10 + 2 + 2 = 17.', 75, 'curated_puzzles', null, null, null),
  ('reasoning', 'Puzzles', 1, 'At a party, each of 10 people shakes hands with every other person exactly once. How many handshakes are there?', array['45', '50', '100', '90'], 0, 'Each handshake is one pair of people: 10 × 9 / 2 = 45.', 40, 'curated_puzzles', null, null, null),
  ('reasoning', 'Puzzles', 3, 'A prize is behind one of 3 doors. You pick a door, and the host, who knows where the prize is, opens another door with no prize behind it. If you switch to the remaining door, what is your chance of winning?', array['1', '1/2', '2/3', '1/3'], 2, 'Your first pick is right only 1/3 of the time. In the other 2/3 of cases the host has to leave the prize behind the remaining door, so switching wins 2/3 of the time.', 75, 'curated_puzzles', null, null, null),
  ('reasoning', 'Puzzles', 2, 'Three ants sit at the corners of a triangle. Each picks one of its two edges at random and walks along it at the same speed. What is the chance that no two ants meet?', array['1/2', '1/8', '1/4', '3/4'], 2, 'There are 2 × 2 × 2 = 8 equally likely choices. The ants avoid each other only if all go clockwise or all go anticlockwise: 2 out of 8, so 1/4.', 55, 'curated_puzzles', null, null, null),
  ('reasoning', 'Puzzles', 2, 'A snail is at the bottom of a 30 m well. Each day it climbs 3 m, and each night it slips back 2 m. On which day does it reach the top?', array['Day 30', 'Day 27', 'Day 28', 'Day 29'], 2, 'After 27 days and nights it is at 27 m. On day 28 it climbs 3 m, reaches 30 m and is out before it can slip back.', 55, 'curated_puzzles', null, null, null),
